CLOSURE_CHECKS_CLASSIFIER_AUDIT: {
  "schema_version": 1,
  "migration_run_id": "17f884f4-9cd3-424e-ab5c-cc1157efaa48",
  "snapshot_timestamp": "2026-05-21T15:55:04Z",
  "ticket_id": "09b0-1d25-1a7d-4cf1",
  "items": [
    {
      "original_text": "SC1: The remediation loop classifies CI check failures into four tiers \u2014 Tier 1 (llm-review, consumes reviewer-findings.json from CI artifacts), Tier 2 (test failures, consumes pytest/jest output from CI artifacts or gh run view logs), Tier 3 (lint/format failures, consumes ruff/eslint output from CI artifacts or logs), Tier 4 (other/unknown, captures check name and raw log tail) \u2014 and dispatches the appropriate fix strategy per tier. Tier definitions and normalizer format are specified in lib/ci-findings-normalize.sh.",
      "original_section": "SC",
      "classification": {
        "label": "end-state",
        "ranking": 5,
        "rationale": "describes a durable system capability for classifying and handling CI failures, phrased as ongoing behavior the system performs"
      },
      "user_decision": "auto",
      "post_migration_section": "SC"
    },
    {
      "original_text": "SC2: The remediation loop detects \"merge conflict\" PR status (via gh pr view --json mergeStateStatus or equivalent) and dispatches /dso:resolve-conflicts before re-triggering CI checks. If conflict resolution fails, the loop emits ESCALATION_REASON: \"Conflict resolution failed\" and exits to user escalation without retrying.",
      "original_section": "SC",
      "classification": {
        "label": "end-state",
        "ranking": 4,
        "rationale": "describes a durable capability of the remediation loop to detect and handle merge conflicts, phrased as ongoing system behavior"
      },
      "user_decision": "auto",
      "post_migration_section": "SC"
    },
    {
      "original_text": "SC3: Each tier maintains an independent attempt counter (ceiling: 5 per tier). A cross-tier regression (e.g., a Tier 1 fix breaks Tier 2) resets the regressed tier's counter to 0. A global ceiling of 15 total attempts across all tiers prevents infinite loops from cross-tier resets. At attempt 4 of any tier, budget-pressure context is injected into the sub-agent prompt (\"This is attempt 4 of 5 \u2014 if you cannot resolve this finding, return ESCALATE with a clear explanation\"). CANNOT_PROCEED is returned if the sub-agent signals it lacks capability. /dso:oscillation-check runs when the same files are targeted on attempt 2+. All counters and phase state persist in the merge-to-main state file for SIGURG resume safety.",
      "original_section": "SC",
      "classification": {
        "label": "end-state",
        "ranking": 4,
        "rationale": "describes durable system behavior for attempt counting, cross-tier regression handling, and state persistence that will remain true after implementation"
      },
      "user_decision": "auto",
      "post_migration_section": "SC"
    },
    {
      "original_text": "SC4: On exhaustion of any tier's ceiling, global ceiling, CANNOT_PROCEED, or oscillation detection, the loop exits with a structured JSON escalation payload written to stdout containing: tiers_attempted (array of tier names), attempts_per_tier (object mapping tier to count), remaining_findings (array of unresolved finding summaries), stop_reason (enum: TIER_CEILING | GLOBAL_CEILING | CANNOT_PROCEED | OSCILLATION | THROTTLE_PAUSE | CONFLICT_RESOLUTION_FAILED | ARTIFACT_MISSING), and suggested_next_step (human-readable string). The script exits with a distinct exit code (exit 2) differentiating remediation exhaustion from pre-remediation failure (exit 1).",
      "original_section": "SC",
      "classification": {
        "label": "end-state",
        "ranking": 4,
        "rationale": "describes durable system behavior and output structure that persists after implementation, though the detailed JSON schema specification edges slightly toward implementation prescription"
      },
      "user_decision": "auto",
      "post_migration_section": "SC"
    },
    {
      "original_text": "SC5: The full remediation loop (poll \u2192 classify \u2192 normalize \u2192 dispatch \u2192 push \u2192 re-poll) runs end-to-end in a sprint session against a branch with an injected CI failure (at least one tier), producing either FIXES_APPLIED with a green CI re-run or a structured escalation payload with correct stop_reason.",
      "original_section": "SC",
      "classification": {
        "label": "end-state",
        "ranking": 4,
        "rationale": "describes a durable system capability (the remediation loop functioning end-to-end) that persists after implementation, phrased as ongoing behavior that the system can perform"
      },
      "user_decision": "auto",
      "post_migration_section": "SC"
    }
  ]
}
